  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Tener \u2014 To Have (One of the Most Important Verbs)",
      "content": "TENER (to have) is irregular and extremely common.\n\nConjugation: yo tengo, t\u00fa tienes, \u00e9l/ella tiene, nosotros tenemos, vosotros ten\u00e9is, ellos tienen\n\nCommon phrases with tener:\n- tengo hambre (I'm hungry)\n- tengo sed (I'm thirsty)\n- tengo fr\u00edo (I'm cold)\n- tengo calor (I'm hot)\n- tengo que... (I have to...)\n- tengo tiempo (I have time)\n- tengo una pregunta (I have a question)\n\nNOTICE: These expressions use TENER, not SER or ESTAR. This is a key difference from English.",
      "examples": [
        "Tengo hambre. (I'm hungry.)\nTengo que irme. (I have to go.)\n\u00bfTienes tiempo? (Do you have time?)",
        "Tenemos una pregunta. (We have a question.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Irregular 'Yo' Forms \u2014 Ir, Dar, Traer, Salir",
      "content": "These common verbs have irregular 'yo' forms in the present tense:\n\nIR (to go): yo voy\nDAR (to give): yo doy\nTRAER (to bring): yo traigo\nSALIR (to leave): yo salgo\nVENIR (to come): yo vengo\nDECIR (to say/tell): yo digo\nCONOCER (to know): yo conozco\nPONGER (to put): yo pongo\nHACER (to make/do): yo hago\n\nThese are essential for travel: 'Voy al hotel', 'Traigo el mapa', 'Salgo ahora'...",
      "examples": [
        "Voy al aeropuerto. (I'm going to the airport.)\nTraigo mi pasaporte. (I'm bringing my passport.)\nSigo mi camino. (I'm leaving my way.)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [
    {
      "title": "B, V, and the Soft G Sound",
      "content": "Spanish has sounds that don't exist in English. The most important for travel:\n\nB/V: Same sound \u2014 a soft 'b' that's more of a lip vibration between vowels\n- buena (BWEH-nah)\n- vaca (VAH-kah) \u2014 same sound as 'b'\n\nG before E/I: Throaty sound like Scottish 'loch'\n- gente (HEN-te)\n- ginger (HEN-ger)\n\nJ: Same as G before E/I\n- jugar (hoo-GAHR)\n- julio (hoo-LYOH)",
      "examples": [
        "buen d\u00eda (bwehn DEE-ah)\nverde (BEHR-deh)\ngente (HEN-te)",
        "juego (HWEH-goh)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "question": "How do you say 'I'm hungry' in Spanish?",
      "options": [
        "Soy hambre",
        "Estoy hambre",
        "Tengo hambre",
        "Hago hambre"
      ],
      "correct": 2,
      "explanation": "'Tengo hambre' = I have hunger = I'm hungry."
    },
    {
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "question": "What is the 'yo' form of 'ir' (to go)?",
      "options": [
        "Yo ir",
        "Yo voy",
        "Yo ido",
        "Yo va"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "'Ir' is irregular: yo voy (I go)."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Tengo ___ y sed.' (I'm hungry and thirsty.)",
      "correct": "hambre",
      "explanation": "'Tengo hambre y sed' = I'm hungry and thirsty."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: '___ que ir al hotel.' (I have to go to the hotel.)",
      "correct": "Tengo",
      "explanation": "'Tengo que' = I have to."
    }
  ]
